# [M6.4.3] D27 blinded corpus -- atomic-groups module.
#
# Atomic groups next to (or wrapping) \K, \G, (?m), and caseless matching.
# The first eight blocks are GOAL_FACTS B.3's own \K/\G/(?i) witnesses,
# independently RE-MEASURED here against libpcre2 10.46 rather than copied
# -- every one reproduced exactly (see README.md's divergence-list section
# for the re-measurement note). python re cannot express \K or \G at all
# (raises "bad escape"), and cannot parse a mid-pattern (?i) the way PCRE2
# does either, so those blocks are marked `# pcre2-only` with the specific
# reason. The remaining LIVE blocks (caseless applied correctly, (?m))
# agree with python re and carry no mark. Three lookaround-beside-a-cut
# blocks that used to live at the end of this file are now commented out
# -- see that section's own note.
#
# Every live block carries `features atomic-groups`; the \K/\G blocks also
# need `assertions` (that is where pcrec gates \K and \G), and a block
# using (?i)/(?m) needs `modifiers` too -- NOT because these are somehow
# extra beyond atomic-groups, but because a block's `features` directive
# REPLACES pcrec's default feature set rather than adding to it (D27
# triage, post-[M6.4.2]: confirmed directly against the CLI -- `--features
# atomic-groups` alone refuses `(?i)...` with "requires module
# 'modifiers'", even though `modifiers` is on by default via std1 when NO
# features directive is given at all). `(?m)` needs a SECOND module on top
# of `modifiers`: the inline `(?m...)` option spelling itself gates on
# `modifiers`, but multiline `^`/`$` anchoring lowers through `assertions`
# -- confirmed directly, `--features atomic-groups,modifiers` alone still
# refuses `(?m)^(?>a+)$` with "inline option 'm' (multiline) requires
# module 'assertions'"; all three together compile it. All of this
# measured directly against the CLI's own "requires module" refusal,
# never assumed from the module names alone.

# \K resets the reported match START, not the atomic group's own
# commitment -- (?>a\Kb) still atomically commits to "ab" as a unit, it
# just reports starting at the \K reset point.
# pcre2-only: python's re module has no \K ("bad escape \K")
pattern (?>a\Kb)c
features atomic-groups,assertions
m "abc" 1 3

# pcre2-only: python bad escape \K
pattern (?>a\Kb|ab)c
features atomic-groups,assertions
m "abc" 1 3

# pcre2-only: python bad escape \K
pattern (?>a|a\Kb)b
features atomic-groups,assertions
m "abb" 0 2

# \K outside the atomic group, atomic group after it: the reset still
# applies to the whole match's reported start.
# pcre2-only: python bad escape \K
pattern a\K(?>b|bc)c
features atomic-groups,assertions
m "abcc" 1 3

# \G ties the atomic group's first alternative to "contiguous with the
# search's own start position" (docs/spec/match_api.md S3.1's find-all
# loop semantics) -- at startpos 0 on "ac", \G is satisfied and the first
# alternative ('a') wins.
# pcre2-only: python bad escape \G
pattern (?>\Ga|b)c
features atomic-groups,assertions
m "ac" 0 2

# On "xbc" (still searched from startpos 0, the default for `m`), \G
# fails everywhere except position 0 -- so the first alternative can only
# ever be tried at position 0 (where it doesn't match 'x'), and the
# leftmost match is via the SECOND alternative at position 1, where \G
# is irrelevant to it.
# pcre2-only: python bad escape \G
pattern (?>\Ga|b)c
features atomic-groups,assertions
m "xbc" 1 3

# \G OUTSIDE the atomic group anchors the whole match to position 0;
# inside, the group commits to 'a' (first alternative), leaving "bc" --
# and 'c' doesn't match 'b'. No fallback to "ab", and \G forbids trying
# any position other than 0, so there is no match anywhere in the
# subject at all.
# pcre2-only: python bad escape \G
pattern \G(?>a|ab)c
features atomic-groups,assertions
n "abc"

# A mid-pattern (?i) inside the FIRST alternative of an atomic group only
# turns on caselessness for that alternative's own remaining scope in
# PCRE2's option-scoping rules -- it does not make the whole atomic group
# (or the literal 'A' the first alternative needs) caseless from the
# group's start, so 'A' still fails to match 'a' and (per the earlier
# trap shape) there is no retry to the second alternative either.
# pcre2-only: python cannot even PARSE a (?i) that is not at the very
# start of the expression ("global flags not at the start of the
# expression")
pattern (?>(?i)a|ab)c
features atomic-groups,modifiers
n "ABc"

# \K used INSIDE the atomic group as literally the last thing in it: the
# group still consumes "aaa" atomically (matching \K's zero-width
# semantics -- it resets the report point but consumes nothing itself),
# reporting an EMPTY span at the point \K fired, then 'b' matches after
# it.
# pcre2-only: python bad escape \K
pattern (?>a+\K)b
features atomic-groups,assertions
m "aaab" 3 4

# --- these agree with python re; no mark needed ---

# (?i) placed correctly (at the pattern's own start) makes the whole
# atomic group caseless, unlike the trap above.
pattern (?i)(?>abc)
features atomic-groups,modifiers
m "ABC" 0 3

# (?m) plus an atomic group: ^ and $ still mean line-start/line-end, and
# the atomic group's own commit-and-refuse behaviour is unaffected by
# multiline mode.
pattern (?m)^(?>a+)$
features atomic-groups,modifiers,assertions
m "aaa\naaa" 0 3

# --- lookaround module is NOT BUILT (D27 triage, post-[M6.4.2]) ---
#
# The three cells below were originally written as ordinary
# atomic-plus-lookaround composition cells. Measured directly against the
# refreshed CLI: `--features atomic-groups,lookaround` on a lookaround
# construct still refuses -- "module 'lookaround' is enabled but (?<...)
# is not implemented yet" -- so no build today can accept them. Left here,
# commented out (not parsed as `pattern` blocks, so run.sh's harness never
# sees them and they are not counted either way), with their intended
# expectations preserved for whoever lands `lookaround` and wants a
# starting point:
#
# pattern (?<=a)(?>b+)c
# features atomic-groups,lookaround
# m "abbbc" 1 5
#
# pattern (?>a+)(?=b)
# features atomic-groups,lookaround
# m "aaab" 0 3
#
# pattern (?>a+)(?!b)
# features atomic-groups,lookaround
# m "aaac" 0 3
